// @run
// @log 3
// @hint redundant type annotation: `p` is inferred as `{x: i32, y: i32}`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `f` is inferred as `i32`
// The annotation on `p` is LOAD-BEARING for this file even though the hint calls it
// redundant: it is the reference that has to resolve to the LOCAL `P`, and the hint's own
// wording is the proof it did — it renders the local shape `{x: i32, y: i32}`, not the
// module-scope `{x: i32}`. Declared rather than deleted, per CLAUDE.md.
// D1045 — A `type` DECLARED INSIDE A FUNCTION BODY IS LEXICALLY SCOPED, and this is the
// SHADOWING spelling: the one face of the defect that was WRONG rather than merely refused.
//
// Before the build the local `P` parsed and was silently DROPPED (type names registered only
// from the module-level walk), so the annotation below resolved to the MODULE-scope `P` and
// the program was told `no field 'y' on P` — about a field the declaration two lines above
// it declares — plus a second complaint that the object literal sets `y`. The interim
// (#2369) collapsed those two diagnostics into one refusal at the declaration; this build
// makes the program RUN, with the local `P` shadowing the module one for the rest of the
// block.
//
// It runs because the parser mints the local declaration under a UNIQUE name (`P` → `P$b1`)
// and spells the annotation at that name, then hoists the declaration into `progStmts` —
// so the checker's `cUserTypes` holds two entries with two shapes and the reference picks
// its own, rather than a scope-aware resolver picking between two `P`s. DECISIONS.md §"A
// `type` declared in a function body is legal and lexically scoped".
//
// STRICTNESS IS THE PIN: the corpus requires every diagnostic to be declared by a directive,
// so a stray `unknown type` or `no field` here fails this file.
type P = { x: i32 }
function f(): i32 {
  type P = { x: i32, y: i32 }
  const p: P = { x: 1, y: 2 }
  return p.x + p.y
}
print(f())
